 - **[Database / State Store] ndjson append logs + atomic JSON snapshots + std `File::lock` on separate `.lock` files (KEYSTONE).** Each event line is written with one `write` call. The next append heals a torn last line; readers count it and never rewrite the log. As landed (chunk 2026-10-10-self-healing-state): every appender (`append_event`, `append_event_at`, `try_append_event`) reads the log's last byte under the lock it already holds and, when the log is not empty and that byte is not LF, writes one LF and its line in a single write. The line then starts on a fresh line, no earlier byte moves, and every offset handed out before still starts the same line; `append_event_at` hands its builder and returns the offset its line really starts at (L + 1 after a heal, L otherwise). The healing process logs one `state-recovered` line, and an append that did not heal logs none. The one reader (`viola_state::events::read_from`: the wrapper's `wait` scan and `last` rebuild, `replay::session_chain`, the pass `viola revive` reads the logged session ids from, and since chunk 2026-10-10-session-links the wrapper's link-set restore at start, `LinkSet::restore`, one pass from offset 0, and since chunk 2026-10-11-the-board-viola-list `viola list`'s `viola_state::board::log_pass`, one pass from offset 0 per scanned instance whose snapshot read or was absent) counts what it steps over, per read, as `unknown_kinds`, `unknown_fields` and `torn_lines`: a line whose `kind` it does not know is not returned (`link` and `unlink` are known kinds since that chunk, returned and no longer counted); a known-kind line with a top-level key outside the six, or a `data` key outside its kind's list, is returned and counted once; an over-long line, a terminated line that is not one JSON object and the unterminated last line of the read count as torn, the last still neither returned nor rewritten. The counts are not logged. `viola list` is the first surface that shows them (chunk 2026-10-11-the-board-viola-list): `list --json`'s `skipped` `{unknown_kinds, unknown_fields, torn_lines}` and the BAY line's `skipped a - b - c`, each summed over every scanned instance, gone ones included; the page's `/api/sessions` reads the same counts in Epoch 8. The UI rebuilds by replaying and tailing. The wheel is decided in the wrapper's memory and mirrored to a snapshot. Budget readings are timestamped, last-writer-wins, under `File::lock`. Rationale: the audit trail stays human-readable and `tail`-able, there is no C in the build, and there is no daemon to serialise writers. SQLite (C build, binary format) and redb (multi-process mode hardened only 2026-09-06) were rejected. Lock files are separate from logs because append + exclusive lock fails on Windows (rust-lang/rust#54118). Upgrade path if a real query need appears: a derived, rebuildable SQLite index (rusqlite) beside the authoritative log. The log stays the source of truth.
-- **[Snapshot writer] tempfile 3.27.0 `persist` through ONE shared helper.** `viola_state::fs::replace_private` creates the temp file beside the target, sets its mode before any byte is written (0600, or 0700 for the pinned `viola`), writes, `sync_all`s and `persist`s; a failure leaves the target as it was. On Windows a `persist` refused with raw OS error 5 (a reader holding the target open, as measured at chunk 2026-09-27-hooks-to-normalised-events research.md M1 under every share mode) is retried every 10 ms, re-persisting the same temp file, at most `viola_state::fs::REPLACE_ATTEMPTS` = 100 attempts (an attempt count, never a time bound); any other error returns at once. `replace_private_shared` is the variant for files every concurrent start of one version writes identically (the pinned copy, the plugin files, the Windows x64 ConPTY companions under `bin/<version>-<hash>/conpty/`): a replace that fails counts as done when the target already holds exactly those bytes. The companions' held open (`FILE_SHARE_READ` only) retries a Win32 error 32 (sharing violation) under the same `REPLACE_ATTEMPTS` bound (`retry_open`, `open_held_waiting`), as measured at chunk 2026-09-29-sideloaded-conpty: two concurrent first starts, the refusal lasting 5-11 ms after the other start's write, 0 of 6 pairs over companions already written. No call site carries its own temp + rename code. Rationale: atomic-write-file 0.3.1 was the first choice, but its licence is BSD-3-Clause, outside `deny.toml`'s allow list (operator ruling at the 2026-09-27-instance-state-and-start-order plan); tempfile was already locked, MIT OR Apache-2.0.
+- **[Snapshot writer] tempfile 3.27.0 `persist` through ONE shared helper.** `viola_state::fs::replace_private` creates the temp file beside the target, sets its mode before any byte is written (0600, or 0700 for the pinned `viola`), writes, `sync_all`s and `persist`s; a failure leaves the target as it was. On Windows a `persist` refused with raw OS error 5 (a reader holding the target open, as measured at chunk 2026-09-27-hooks-to-normalised-events research.md M1 under every share mode) is retried every 10 ms, re-persisting the same temp file, at most `viola_state::fs::REPLACE_ATTEMPTS` = 100 attempts (an attempt count, never a time bound); any other error returns at once. The loop sits in the private `replace_private_retrying`, which takes the decision to retry a failed rename (from its raw OS error) from its caller; `replace_private_with` passes `retry_replace(_, HOST_IS_WINDOWS)`, so the product's behaviour is the one above and the loop's attempt bound is exercised by a unit case on every host (chunk 2026-10-13-epoch-4-survivors). `replace_private_shared` is the variant for files every concurrent start of one version writes identically (the pinned copy, the plugin files, the Windows x64 ConPTY companions under `bin/<version>-<hash>/conpty/`): a replace that fails counts as done when the target already holds exactly those bytes. The companions' held open (`FILE_SHARE_READ` only) retries a Win32 error 32 (sharing violation) under the same `REPLACE_ATTEMPTS` bound (`retry_open`, `open_held_waiting`), as measured at chunk 2026-09-29-sideloaded-conpty: two concurrent first starts, the refusal lasting 5-11 ms after the other start's write, 0 of 6 pairs over companions already written. No call site carries its own temp + rename code. Rationale: atomic-write-file 0.3.1 was the first choice, but its licence is BSD-3-Clause, outside `deny.toml`'s allow list (operator ruling at the 2026-09-27-instance-state-and-start-order plan); tempfile was already locked, MIT OR Apache